detail.abstract:
Why do constitutionally equivalent phrases produce radically di4erent jurisprudential outcomes? Argentine Supreme Court jurisprudence (1922-2025) reveals "general welfare" (bienestar general) and "common good" (bien común)-philosophically synonymous phrases-evolved a 17.8-fold fitness di4erential. Bienestar general dominates democratic periods (JurisRank 0.89, 60+ cases) while bien común achieved maximum fitness only during Catholic-military dictatorships (1943-1983, G-score 0.70), collapsing fourteen-fold following democratic transition despite unchanged constitutional text. Traditional theories cannot explain this pattern.&nbsp; 
<div>
 Textualism predicts identical treatment of synonyms. Originalism expects continuity regardless of regime type. Extended Phenotype Theory (Dawkins 1982) and Evolutionary Stable Strategies (Maynard Smith 1973) explain this divergence: constitutional language functions as genetic code for institutional construction rather than semantic constraint.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 The divergence reflects niche specialization. Bienestar general evolved fitness in democratic regimes through genealogical link to U.S. New Deal jurisprudence (Avico 1934 citing Blaisdell), expanding via Peralta (1990) and Badaro (2007, 50+ subsequent citations). Bien común evolved fitness in authoritarian regimes through papal encyclicals requiring specific machinery (mandatory Catholic education, corporatist organization, Borda's 1968 Civil Code).&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 When democracy dismantled this machinery (1983-1987), bien común collapsed. Cross-national analysis (n=47) confirms vague emergency language increases constitutional mutation odds 17.6-fold (p&lt;0.001). Institutional friction (Switzerland's direct democracy, Canada's automatic sunsets) prevents mutation. I derive five design principles for mutationresistant clauses and demonstrate Justice Barra's 1991 state-centric formulation had predicted negative fitness (G=-0.636), correctly forecasting zero citations and reversal (ALITT 2006). Constitutional fitness depends on systemic context, not philosophical foundations.
</div>
